This is the license for Reactive TUI. It is the MIT License with two
restrictions. The first applies only if you offer Reactive TUI itself,
or a copy of it, to other developers as a framework (Section 2). The
second applies only to organizations that earn money with Reactive TUI
and have revenue above USD 1,000,000 a year (Section 3).


PLAIN-LANGUAGE SUMMARY (informative; the numbered terms below control)

  If you are a person, or an organization with revenue of USD 1,000,000
  a year or less, and you build applications with Reactive TUI, this
  license is the MIT License. Build them, ship them, sell them. No fee
  and no permission are needed. The same holds for a larger
  organization that earns no money from what it builds with it.

  Restriction one: nobody may take Reactive TUI, or a copy of it that
  was changed or renamed, and offer it to developers as a framework for
  building terminal applications, unless the Licensor gave written
  permission. See Section 2.

  Restriction two: an organization that earns money from software
  built with Reactive TUI, and has revenue above USD 1,000,000 a year,
  must sponsor the project. See Section 3.


1.  PERMISSIVE GRANT

  Reactive TUI is licensed under the MIT License (full text in
  LICENSE-MIT, also at <https://opensource.org/licenses/MIT>), EXCEPT
  as narrowed by the Framework Rider in Section 2 and the Sponsorship
  Rider in Section 3. For every use that neither rider covers, the MIT
  License applies with no further condition and no fee.


2.  THE FRAMEWORK RIDER

2.1  What is covered

  A "Copy" is Reactive TUI or any substantial part of it, in source or
  compiled form, changed or not, under its own name or another.

  A "Framework Offering" is any library, crate, package, software
  development kit, product or service that both:

    (i)  contains a Copy; and

    (ii) is published, distributed or otherwise offered to developers
         outside your own organization as a means of building terminal
         user interfaces or terminal applications.

  It does not matter whether a Framework Offering is free or paid, or
  under which license it is offered.

  You need a Framework License, granted in writing by the Licensor, to
  publish, distribute or operate a Framework Offering.

2.2  What is not covered

  The following are not Framework Offerings, and Section 1 covers them:

    - any application built with Reactive TUI, sold or free, with
      Reactive TUI inside it in source or compiled form;

    - a changed Copy that is used inside one organization only;

    - a changed Copy that is published only to propose its changes to
      the Reactive TUI project, and is not presented as a framework of
      its own;

    - a library of widgets, themes, language bindings or integrations
      that uses Reactive TUI as a dependency, holds no Copy, and names
      Reactive TUI as what it is built on;

    - redistribution of Reactive TUI, unchanged and under its own name
      and this license, by a package registry, a mirror or an operating
      system distribution.

2.3  Framework License grants

  The Licensor is under no obligation to grant a Framework License and
  may set any terms for one. Requests go to the address in Section 9.

  If you are not sure whether what you build is a Framework Offering,
  ask the Licensor before you publish it. Where this rider is unclear,
  it is read in favor of the permissive grant, unless the use is
  plainly a Framework Offering.


3.  THE SPONSORSHIP RIDER

3.1  Who is covered

  An "Organization" is a company or other legal entity together with
  its Affiliates. An "Affiliate" is any entity that controls it, is
  controlled by it, or is under common control with it, where control
  means more than half of the ownership or of the voting rights.

  "Revenue" is the gross revenue of the Organization from all sources
  in its last completed fiscal year, in US dollars at the exchange rate
  of the last day of that year.

  A "Commercial Product" is software built with Reactive TUI that the
  Organization earns money from: software that it sells, licenses for
  a fee or offers as a paid service, or that is part of a product or a
  service it is paid for.

  A "Covered Organization" is an Organization whose Revenue is above
  USD 1,000,000 and that offers a Commercial Product.

3.2  The rule

  A Covered Organization must sponsor the Reactive TUI project for as
  long as it offers a Commercial Product.

  To sponsor the project is to pay it a recurring sponsorship through
  the sponsorship page named in Section 9. The level is the sponsor's
  choice among the levels the project offers there, unless the
  Organization and the Licensor agree on another in writing.

  A Commercial Product that a person or another organization builds
  for a Covered Organization, under contract or as an employee, is the
  Covered Organization's.

3.3  What stays free

  No sponsorship is owed by:

    - a person who acts for themselves and not for a Covered
      Organization;

    - an Organization whose Revenue is USD 1,000,000 or less;

    - an Organization that earns no money from software built with
      Reactive TUI, for example one that uses such software only
      inside its own business or gives it away;

    - a school, a university, or a registered non-profit organization,
      whatever its revenue;

    - anybody who only runs an application that another party built
      with Reactive TUI;

    - anybody who works on Reactive TUI itself in order to contribute
      to the project.

3.4  When the sponsorship begins

  A Covered Organization begins to sponsor the project within 90 days
  after it first offered a Commercial Product or, if that is later,
  after the end of the fiscal year in which its Revenue passed the
  limit.

3.5  Other terms

  A Covered Organization that cannot sponsor the project in this way
  may ask the Licensor for a written license on other terms.


